-- title:
--   Proposition 3.5 — the discretised kernel dominates a cone kernel
-- statement:
--   For $d \ge 2$, $0 < \vartheta \le \pi/2$ and $0 < \alpha \le 2$ there are a
--   constant $C > 0$ and an angle $\theta' \in (0,\pi/2]$ — **uniform in
--   everything that follows** — such that for every $\vartheta$-bounded
--   configuration $\Gamma$ satisfying `CondMeas`, and every $\Lambda$ and kernel
--   $k$ with the bounds (2), there is a **new** configuration $\Gamma'$, bounded by
--   $\theta'$, with: for all lattice points $x,y$ separated by more than $\sqrt d$,
--
--   $$\frac{C}{\Lambda}\Bigl(\mathbf 1[y \in V^{\Gamma'}\![x]] + \mathbf 1[x \in V^{\Gamma'}\![y]]\Bigr)\,
--   \|x-y\|^{-d-\alpha}
--   \;\le\;
--   \int_{Q(x)\times Q(y)} k ,$$
--
--   the right side being the mass of $k$ over the open unit cubes at $x$ and $y$.
--
--   $C$ and $\theta'$ depend only on $d$, $\vartheta$, $\alpha$; $\Gamma'$ is chosen
--   after $\Gamma$, $\Lambda$ and $k$ but **before** $x$ and $y$, so one $\Gamma'$
--   serves all admissible lattice pairs.
--
--   Note $\alpha \le 2$ is **non-strict** here — $\alpha = 2$ is permitted, unlike
--   in Theorem 1.1. Nothing is asserted for pairs at distance $\le \sqrt d$, or for
--   non-lattice points, and the bound is one-sided: no upper bound on the
--   discretised kernel is claimed.
--
--   What is *not* said about $\Gamma'$: it is related to $\Gamma$ **only** through
--   the displayed inequality. It is not required to be measurable, nor to have
--   cones contained in or containing those of $\Gamma$; and $\theta'$ is not
--   required to be below or above $\vartheta$ — only to lie in $(0,\pi/2]$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/dbenbenn/quadratic-forms-sobolev/blob/7a1a680db2124d46ce370c91fd450aa454edf491/QuadraticFormsSobolev/Section3Kernel.lean#L320-L437

